{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Evolutionary survival implies adaptation to a real environment; Popperian corroboration explicitly denies implication of truth.","Sports淘汰 (elimination) aims to find a winner; scientific falsification aims to keep the contest open indefinitely.","Legal 'innocent until proven guilty' is a moral safeguard; Popper's rule is an epistemic accelerator."],"overturn_conditions":["If it can be shown that 'severity' itself presupposes an inductive assumption about the uniformity of nature.","If acting on a theory logically entails believing it will succeed, making the 'non-belief' stance performative nonsense."],"shared_structure":["Selection based on elimination of competitors rather than positive confirmation.","Procedural rules governing a game vs. ontological claims about the game pieces.","Preference for the 'fittest' survivor without assuming the environment remains static."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_003","SRC_012"],"transfer_claims":["Just as we bet on the undefeated boxer not because they are invincible but because they are the only remaining viable candidate, we act on corroborated theories because they are the only ones not yet eliminated.","The rationality of the choice lies in the structure of the competition (severe testing), not in the properties of the survivor."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Popper's preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on his own terms as a non-inductive methodological rule of 'critical rationality,' but accepting this defense costs the scientist any claim to predictive reliability or increased truth-probability, reducing the preference to a purely procedural commitment to keep the game of science going rather than a guide to nature's behavior.","counterconditions":["If surviving severe tests is treated as evidence of future success, the argument collapses into induction.","If the preference relies on an undefined notion of 'verisimilitude' that cannot be formally assessed, the rationality claim becomes empty.","If the 'methodological rule' is shown to be functionally identical to inductive practice in guiding action, the distinction is merely verbal."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The mechanism distinguishes between 'justification' (which implies induction/probability) and 'rational choice' (which implies selecting the best available target for criticism). By framing the preference as a rule to maximize the severity of future potential falsifications, the choice becomes logical (choose the theory with more empirical content) rather than inductive (choose the theory likely to be true).","optional_refs":["8894e8a94315eb8211da75e68aa0fbf10e3882969b719481670d8a0c80da99c5","48beb42152136a8e047c5f93d681c5d8e9862a573960b35c2c4f0068bbde5f67"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["8894e8a94315eb8211da75e68aa0fbf10e3882969b719481670d8a0c80da99c5","48beb42152136a8e047c5f93d681c5d8e9862a573960b35c2c4f0068bbde5f67"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.75}],"simulation_proposals":[]}